"} {"question" "q-15422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{6x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{e}^{6x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(6{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{6x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{e}^{6x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(6{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6J \\\\ I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6(-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-36I \\\\ 37I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{37}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050879/state.json" "file_sha256" "874fe499780830018c708f2824d31e0038879f10565d701af8b059efa6657e2f"} {"question" "q-15422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050879/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d51f9ac6e40cd86e174554bbafa9211337125098e606e5510a0bcb7968fe6bb"} {"question" "q-15422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050879/state.json" "file_sha256" "874fe499780830018c708f2824d31e0038879f10565d701af8b059efa6657e2f"} {"question" "q-15422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{26}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{26}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{26}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{37}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{37}+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050879/state.json" "file_sha256" "874fe499780830018c708f2824d31e0038879f10565d701af8b059efa6657e2f"} {"question" "q-15422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{37}+C"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050879/state.json" "file_sha256" "874fe499780830018c708f2824d31e0038879f10565d701af8b059efa6657e2f"} {"question" "q-50013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050879/state.json" "file_sha256" "874fe499780830018c708f2824d31e0038879f10565d701af8b059efa6657e2f"} {"question" "q-50013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2(\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{{e}^{-2(-\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi }}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050879/state.json" "file_sha256" "874fe499780830018c708f2824d31e0038879f10565d701af8b059efa6657e2f"} {"question" "q-50013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/ques